 ## Overview
-The Möller-Trumbore algorithm computes the intersection of a ray with a triangle by solving for the parametric distance $t$ and barycentric coordinates $(u, v)$ directly, avoiding the explicit calculation of the triangle's plane equation [S3, S10, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from general analytic methods by using Cramer's rule to derive these parameters in a single step, which minimizes storage requirements and computational overhead [S10, unofficial]. Unlike [General Ray-Triangle Intersection (Plücker Coordinates)](909-general-ray-triangle-intersection-pl-cker-coordinates.md), which relies on Plücker coordinates to handle arbitrary ray directions with a different algebraic structure, Möller-Trumbore operates directly in Cartesian space using cross and dot products [S8, unofficial].
+The Möller-Trumbore algorithm computes the intersection of a ray with a triangle by solving for the parametric distance $t$ and barycentric coordinates $(u, v)$ directly, avoiding the explicit calculation of the triangle's plane equation [S3, unofficial][S10, unofficial]. It translates the ray origin and changes the vector basis to yield a vector $(t, u, v)^T$, minimizing storage requirements and computational overhead [S11, unofficial]. It operates directly in Cartesian space using cross and dot products [S8, unofficial].
 
